   claim:
     "The akasha mechanical landing road never pushes, so the migration's own commits hand off nothing. The two pushes on 2026-09-03 at 13:58 were `git push origin <sha>:main` typed by an agent, because `akasha deploy` refuses with that exact command as its remedy and `block-destructive-git` allows an unforced push. Under a swarm committing every few seconds the sha is stale before the deploy rechecks it, so the remedy becomes a retry loop, and each try carries the whole branch. 5,848 commits reached the remote that way.",
   evidence:
-    "Measured 2026-09-03 at HEAD f396253b7e.\n\nWHAT PUSHES. `pushBranch` (akasha/git/git-pushing/git-pushing.module.code.ts:45) runs `git push <remote> HEAD:refs/heads/<branch>`. Its only caller is repo/push/push-repo.ts:82,85, spawned detached by `handOffPush` (akasha/git/git-push-handoff/git-push-handoff.module.code.ts:128), whose only caller is akasha/command-system/harness-landing/harness-landing.module.code.ts:356. The path to the pusher is built at runtime at git-push-handoff:125, so no import edge names it.\n\nTHE MIGRATION ROAD DOES NOT REACH IT. `rg --no-ignore --hidden 'harness-landing|handOffPush'` over akasha/command-system/asking/ and akasha/command-system/landing/ exits 1 with no match, against a positive control on a token I knew was there. So `landedMechanically` cannot push, and the seat writes the migration makes every few seconds push nothing.\n\nNOT ATTEMPTED, RATHER THAN ATTEMPTED AND FAILED. `carry()` in push-repo.ts writes `.git/harness-push/main.state` on both outcomes: `ok:true` on success and `ok:false` with a reason after one retry. That file has held `ok:true` for 6842c62c45 since 2026-09-02 21:45:59 and its mtime has not moved in the 16 hours since, while main advanced by thousands of commits. A pusher failing repeatedly would have rewritten it with `ok:false`; a stuck lock would leave a main.lock, and there is none. I confirmed `writePushState` works by round-tripping it against a scratch repo, and confirmed the pusher still loads by running it against a scratch repo with no remote, where `remoteOf` answers null before any push: exit 0. The control, the same file at the same location with one specifier repointed at a missing module, exits 1 with `Cannot find module`.\n\nWHO PUSHED. `.git/logs/refs/remotes/origin/main` records `update by push` at 1788465492 and 1788465507, which only the sending repository writes, so both ran in this checkout. The transcript at /var/home/walton/.claude/projects/-var-home-walton-repos/f0d72724-6f3b-423c-945a-a650330a480b.jsonl line 2445 holds the deploy refusal, exit 3: `origin main does not carry 234aaeaf35..., and a pod serves what origin carries, so run `git push origin 234aaeaf35...:main` and deploy again`. Line 2507 is `git push origin \"$SHA:main\"`. Line 2512 is the same refusal again, on a newer sha. Line 2515 is a five-round loop of push-then-deploy that exits only when the refusal stops naming origin. The refusal text is composed at akasha/command-system/commands/deploy/web-putting-up/deploy-web-putting-up.module.code.ts:77, and `carriedByOrigin` (akasha/infrastructure/cluster/services/web-app-building/web-app-building.module.code.ts:84) runs `git fetch origin main`, which wrote .git/FETCH_HEAD one second after the second push.\n\nWHY IT LOOKED LIKE A SEAT WRITE. A push carries whatever HEAD names at that instant, not the commit its caller made, and subagent pages are written often enough that both push heads were seat commits by coincidence.\n\nTHE OLD CADENCE. Until 2026-09-02 21:45 the remote was updated every fifteen minutes, a few seconds after a `message to akasha from akasha` commit: the reminder delivery landing through the harness road. 67c131b727 at 21:45:05 carries no `Checks-bypassed` trailer; every such commit from c37dc1b1d2 at 22:40:03 onward carries one. Message writing moved onto the mechanical road in that window, and the cadence ended with it. 194 message commits since have pushed nothing.\n\nREACH. `git ls-remote origin refs/heads/main` answers e455097e7a, and `git rev-list --count 6842c62c45..e455097e7a` is 5,848. origin/main is an ancestor of HEAD, so nothing is on the remote that is not here. akasha/git-transport/hooks/post-receive-mirror mirrors a receiving repo onward with `--force --prune` where that repo declares `mirror.url`; whether alan/akasha declares one is server-side and I could not read it.",
